      "question": "What can strict mode be enabled on, and what happens instead of coercing an incorrect type?",
      "before_sources": [
        {
          "path_or_url": "docs/concepts/strict_mode.md",
          "snippet": "However, there are also situations where this is not desirable, and you want Pydantic to error instead of coercing data.\n\nTo better support this use case, Pydantic provides a \"strict mode\" that can be enabled on a per-model, per-field, or\neven per-validation-call basis. When strict mode is enabled, Pydantic will be much less lenient when coercing data,\nand will instead error if the data is not of the correct type.",
          "line_start": 9,
          "line_end": 13
        }
      ],
      "after_sources": [
        {
          "path_or_url": "docs/concepts/strict_mode.md",
          "snippet": "However, there are also situations where this is not desirable, and you want Pydantic to error instead of coercing data.\n\nTo better support this use case, Pydantic provides a \"strict mode\" that can be enabled on a per-model, per-field, or\neven per-validation-call basis. When strict mode is enabled, Pydantic will be much less lenient when coercing data,\nand will instead error if the data is not of the correct type.",
          "line_start": 9,
          "line_end": 13
        }
      ]
